WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.189
32 hashing tasks, with no I/O and no locks, on a machine with four cores.

2
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.481
One thread works through them in 359 ms.

3
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:15.264
Two threads take 171.

4
00:00:15.264 --> 00:00:16.852
Four take 92.

5
00:00:17.067 --> 00:00:19.164
So how long will eight threads take?

6
00:00:21.367 --> 00:00:22.157
96 ms.

7
00:00:22.157 --> 00:00:23.474
No faster.

8
00:00:23.474 --> 00:00:26.635
Sixteen threads take 98.

9
00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:29.046
A core runs one thread at a time.

10
00:00:29.046 --> 00:00:33.139
Thread five only gets a core by taking it from another thread.

11
00:00:33.139 --> 00:00:35.847
That switch costs time, and adds no work.

12
00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:42.644
This is CPU-bound work: about one thread per core, the number availableProcessors returns.

13
00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:45.708
Most backend work waits.

14
00:00:45.708 --> 00:00:52.085
An authorization computes for 20 ms, then waits 180 ms for the card issuer.

15
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.990
While it waits, the thread needs no CPU.

16
00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:57.881
The core sits idle nine tenths of the time.

17
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:01.264
How many threads does it take to keep this one core busy?

18
00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:04.171
About ten.

19
00:01:04.171 --> 00:01:09.803
The wait is nine times the compute, so while one thread computes, nine can wait.

20
00:01:09.803 --> 00:01:11.423
This is I/O-bound work.

21
00:01:12.333 --> 00:01:14.872
Goetz's formula says the same.

22
00:01:14.872 --> 00:01:18.427
Eight cores at 80 percent CPU: 64 threads.

23
00:01:19.233 --> 00:01:26.771
From the traffic side: 400 authorizations arrive each second, and each one stays 250 ms.

24
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.161
How many are inside at any moment?

25
00:01:31.367 --> 00:01:32.728
Follow the first one.

26
00:01:32.728 --> 00:01:36.033
While it crosses, new ones keep arriving behind it.

27
00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:39.792
When it leaves, 100 are inside, and the count stays there.

28
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.657
This is Little's law.

29
00:01:41.657 --> 00:01:45.445
In flight equals arrival rate times time inside.

30
00:01:45.445 --> 00:01:48.128
400 times a quarter second is 100.

31
00:01:48.128 --> 00:01:50.416
One thread each: 100 threads.

32
00:01:51.933 --> 00:01:53.000
Turn it around.

33
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:59.541
With only 50 threads, each finishing four requests a second, the pool finishes 200 a second.

34
00:01:59.767 --> 00:02:00.842
But 400 arrive.

35
00:02:00.842 --> 00:02:08.015
Each second, the other 200 wait in the queue, and the queue grows until memory or patience runs out.

36
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.495
Threads that compute: one per core.

37
00:02:11.495 --> 00:02:14.916
Threads that wait: arrival rate times time inside.

38
00:02:15.133 --> 00:02:16.390
Check yourself.

39
00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:19.910
20 threads, and each request takes 100 ms.

40
00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:22.172
How many requests a second?

41
00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:25.243
200.

42
00:02:25.243 --> 00:02:28.156
Each thread finishes ten a second.
